AB  - The use of Spring Boot microservices in modern software structures is a contributor to the increase in attack surface as well the need to have a high level of security measures that would go beyond the traditional firewalls. This paper explores how artificial intelligence can be applied to identify advanced API attacks like credential stuffing, injection attacks, and broken object-level authorization. To address this, we suggest an advanced AI-based layer be used to monitor traffic patterns and detect anomalies that are not easily detected by rule-based systems. The study applies a selected set of 397 samples of network traffic logs, including both benign and malicious API calls. The main tools used in the present research are the Spring Boot platform on microservice orchestration, Python to write the machine learning models, and Scikit-learn to implement the detection algorithms. The findings show that the AI-based solution has a considerable decrease in the false positive and high rate of detection of zero-day vulnerabilities. Through automated threat hunting, the system will offer a proactive defense system that is responsive to changing attack vectors. This paper has determined that the integration of intelligence into the API gateway layer of a Spring boot ecosystem is a sure way of providing a more secure and scalable security posture in enterprise level applications.
ER  - 

