AB  - Modern microservice systems face multidimensional runtime uncertainties ranging from request-pattern bursts and latency spikes to nondeterministic AI component outputs and retry amplification storms. Existing resilience-testing approaches—including classical fault injection and rule-based chaos engineering—focus on single-service failures and predefined disruption templates, leaving cross-service uncertainty propagation substantially under-explored. We propose UncertainFuzz, a system-level fuzzing framework that uses a large language model (LLM) to synthesize multi-stage uncertainty campaigns: coherent sequences of correlated perturbations drawn from service topology, historical incidents, and SLO definitions. A lightweight injector instantiates each campaign across a live benchmark cluster, while a graph-based evaluator measures fault-propagation depth and resilience score. We evaluate UncertainFuzz on DeathStarBench and Google Online Boutique against three baselines. UncertainFuzz discovers 56% more new failure paths per campaign and achieves 86% aggregate SLO-violation coverage after ten campaigns, compared to 62% for rule-based chaos and 48% for random fault injection. Ablation experiments confirm that LLM conditioning and topology-aware planning each contribute measurably, though gains are more modest when historical incident data are sparse.
ER  - 

