AB  - Purpose] Under the deep convergence of global economic integration and the fourth industrial revolution, the automotive manufacturing industry is undergoing a profound paradigm shift. Traditional legacy systems expose serious vulnerabilities and the “bullwhip effect” within complex multi-level global networks. [Methodology] To address these issues and bridge the theoretical gap in interdisciplinary technological integration, this study constructs a full-stack cloud-based supply chain management (SCM) platform architecture. It fundamentally synergizes artificial intelligence (multimodal LSTM, PSO), the Internet of Things, knowledge graphs, and digital twins. The proposed approach is rigorously evaluated through a hybrid simulation framework encompassing system dynamics and discrete event modeling. [Findings] The simulation results demonstrate that this digital paradigm significantly increases the network visibility of Tier-2 suppliers from $\mathbf{1 4. 2} \%$ to $\mathbf{8 7. 6} \%$. Furthermore, it structurally reduces the total global operating costs by 30.0% and compresses production line downtime to zero during extreme supply disruption stress tests. Ultimately, this framework provides both theoretical innovations and actionable guidelines for automotive enterprises to achieve optimal system resilience.
ER  - 

