AB  - The growth of Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices and smart sensors has transformed the data collection and automated decision-making capabilities in various fields, from industrial automation to environmental monitoring. However, with this rapid growth comes considerable risk due to vulnerabilities where spoofed sensors can place false data into mission-critical systems. Most traditional fault detection techniques rely on rule based thresholds or human-defined heuristic rules that cannot adapt to changing attack vectors and complex, high-dimensional datasets in the same way that a human reviews a practical environment. The contribution of this work is a unified machine learning framework for real-time fault detection in IoT-enabled smart sensor networks, using ensemble and deep-learning models constructed for deployment in cloud environments. The framework will include feature extraction, model training, and real-time inference and automated response modules that will allow for fast isolation and mitigation of anomalies. Evaluation of the approach on a simulated smart factory dataset shows that this approach is able to detect both abrupt and gradual sensor faults with an F1-score of 0.94, which is 12% improvement over baseline methods. Finally, the integration with cloud orchestration tools, allows for a scalable solution for the deployment of multiple model instances along with dynamic resource management for the purpose of ensuring low-latency responses at varying loads.
ER  - 

